trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Absence of GI symptoms (including melena, hematochezia, or altered bowel habits) makes occult GI malignancy less likely — though not impossible (e.g., early-stage colon cancer may be silent). Next, fatigue + weight loss strongly warrant evaluation for endocrine dysfunction (e.g., hyperthyroidism — which can cause weight loss *despite* increased appetite and fatigue; or adrenal insufficiency), hematologic causes (e.g., anemia, myelodysplasia), or chronic inflammatory conditions. Given the chronicity and systemic nature, initial laboratory screening is now essential to guide further workup. The most efficient first-tier tests are:  
- Complete blood count (CBC) to assess for anemia, leukopenia/thrombocytopenia (suggesting bone marrow involvement or chronic disease), or abnormal cell morphology;  
- Comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP) to evaluate electrolytes, renal/hepatic function, calcium (elevated in malignancy or granulomatous disease), and glucose (for diabetes);  
- Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) to rule out thyroid dysfunction — a very common, treatable cause of fatigue and weight change.  
These three tests together provide broad coverage with high diagnostic yield and low cost. I will request them as a single coordinated test panel.

[TEST] Complete blood count (CBC) with differential, comprehensive metabolic panel (CMP), and thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH).

